WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.520
Nuclear receptors are proteins inside the cell that catch chemical signals and

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.100
control whether genes are copied into RNA.

3
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.780
The receptor itself is a transcription factor.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.440
That matters because one receptor can reset many genes at once.

5
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.820
New RNA and protein take time to make, so the response builds over hours.

6
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.700
But the new proteins remain after the hormone has gone.

7
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.740
A steroid hormone crosses the membrane to reach its receptor.

8
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.110
The receptor is one chain with two grips.

9
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.390
One region binds the hormone.

10
00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.250
Another recognizes a specific short stretch of DNA.

11
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.330
Most nuclear receptors work in pairs, and their DNA binding site is called a

12
00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:53.650
response element. The glucocorticoid receptor pairs with another copy of itself

13
00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:54.870
after hormone arrives.

14
00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:00.890
The thyroid hormone receptor waits on DNA with a different partner, called RXR.

15
00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.550
Hormone recognition and DNA recognition are separate jobs in the same protein.

16
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:12.310
Hormone binding changes the receptor's shape and which helper proteins it holds.

17
00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:18.270
The thyroid hormone receptor releases a co-repressor and brings in a co-activator.

18
00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.270
These helpers act on chromatin, the packaged form of DNA.

19
00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:28.810
Co-activators bring enzymes that add acetyl groups to histones, the proteins DNA

20
00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.570
wraps around. That loosens the packing and helps RNA polymerase start.

21
00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.230
Co-repressors bring enzymes that remove those groups.

22
00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:40.270
The cell's own mix of helpers also matters.

23
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.853
The family reaches beyond familiar hormones.

24
00:01:44.753 --> 00:01:47.073
Some receptors sense the cell's own lipids.

25
00:01:47.953 --> 00:01:49.413
PPARs bind fatty acids.

26
00:01:50.393 --> 00:01:54.833
LXR binds oxidized cholesterol, and FXR binds bile acids.

27
00:01:55.713 --> 00:01:58.253
PPAR-gamma drives the making of fat cells.

28
00:01:59.193 --> 00:02:03.773
So an intracellular signal can connect to gene control in metabolism, too.

29
00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:06.547
That same route meets medicine.

30
00:02:07.507 --> 00:02:12.227
Prednisone and dexamethasone act through the glucocorticoid receptor to calm

31
00:02:12.227 --> 00:02:17.167
inflammation. Long treatment also raises blood glucose and thins bone.

32
00:02:18.127 --> 00:02:19.587
Tissue context matters, too.

33
00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:25.327
Tamoxifen blocks estrogen in the breast but imitates it in the uterus and, after

34
00:02:25.327 --> 00:02:26.527
menopause, in bone.

35
00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:32.607
Its receptor still binds DNA, and the switching surface called AF-1 can still work.

36
00:02:32.607 --> 00:02:36.947
What happens next can depend on the helper proteins each tissue makes.

37
00:02:37.907 --> 00:02:42.027
The signal, the receptor and the cell's helpers together shape gene control.
